 then Unicode whitespace collapse, parsed JSON text turns; null canonicalizes to empty string and empty turns are conservatively hashed/grouped. Group ANY shared prompt/response turn or normalized raw example BEFORE endpoint/outcome extraction. NBA normalized date, sorted original team codes and aligned scores, only _iscopy=0.\",\n  \"cross_plane_content_intersection\": 0,\n  \"cross_plane_source_id_intersection\": 0,\n  \"own_hash_commitment\": \"0220b0dbda5657255ebd802bf98576d752998eb3ce74fffd63d27f05fed83f50\",\n  \"peer_hash_commitment\": \"5131a30078c28ba6365b5e2459ef8066e704f4a16c0eddcb9f1251babe3f9ffe\",\n  \"raw_rows_in_partition\": 44316,\n  \"retained_rows\": 41418,\n  \"duplicates_removed\": 0,\n  \"excluded_rows\": 2898,\n  \"components\": [\n    [\n      \"ANA\",\n      \"CAR\",\n      \"DLC\",\n      \"DNA\",\n      \"DNR\",\n      \"FLO\",\n      \"HSM\",\n      \"INA\",\n      \"KEN\",\n      \"LAS\",\n      \"MMF\",\n      \"MMP\",\n      \"MMS\",\n      \"MMT\",\n      \"MNM\",\n      \"MNP\",\n      \"NJA\",\n      \"NOB\",\n      \"NYA\",\n      \"OAK\",\n      \"PTC\",\n      \"PTP\",\n      \"SAA\",\n      \"SDA\",\n      \"SDS\",\n      \"SSL\",\n      \"TEX\",\n      \"UTS\",\n      \"VIR\",\n      \"WSA\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"AND\",\n      \"ATL\",\n      \"BAL\",\n      \"BLB\",\n      \"BOS\",\n      \"BRK\",\n      \"BUF\",\n      \"CAP\",\n      \"CHA\",\n      \"CHH\",\n      \"CHI\",\n      \"CHO\",\n      \"CHP\",\n      \"CHS\",\n      \"CHZ\",\n      \"CIN\",\n      \"CLE\",\n      \"CLR\",\n      \"DAL\",\n      \"DEN\",\n      \"DET\",\n      \"DNN\",\n      \"DTF\",\n      \"FTW\",\n      \"GSW\",\n      \"HOU\",\n      \"IND\",\n      \"INJ\",\n      \"INO\",\n      \"KCK\",\n      \"KCO\",\n      \"LAC\",\n      \"LAL\",\n      \"MEM\",\n      \"MIA\",\n      \"MIL\",\n      \"MIN\",\n      \"MLH\",\n      \"MNL\",\n      \"NJN\",\n      \"NOH\",\n      \"NOJ\",\n      \"NOK\",\n      \"NOP\",\n      \"NYK\",\n      \"NYN\",\n      \"OKC\",\n      \"ORL\",\n      \"PHI\",\n      \"PHO\",\n      \"PHW\",\n      \"PIT\",\n      \"POR\",\n      \"PRO\",\n      \"ROC\",\n      \"SAC\",\n      \"SAS\",\n      \"SDC\",\n      \"SDR\",\n      \"SEA\",\n      \"SFW\",\n      \"SHE\",\n      \"STB\",\n      \"STL\",\n      \"SYR\",\n      \"TOR\",\n      \"TRH\",\n      \"TRI\",\n      \"UTA\",\n      \"VAN\",\n      \"WAS\",\n      \"WAT\",\n      \"WSB\",\n      \"WSC\"\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"limitations\": \"Partition-specific largest undirected component; NBA historical team codes and eras heterogeneous. No causal evaluation-design comparison. Raw originals staged privately under /tmp at draft time, never runtime inputs.\"\n}\nnumpy==2.2.6\nscipy==1.15.3\n\"\"\"CPU influence-guided comparison deletion; finite JSON handle(request) protocol.\n\nEvery request is a dict with op below. Responses have EXACTLY the documented\nkeys; no additional fields. No paths or external state are accepted or returned.\nAll seeds are arbitrary nonnegative integers; they select RNG, never workload.\n\nreset {seed:int} -> {ok:true}: clear ALL buffers, certificates and tokens.\nexport_chunk {token:str,index:int} -> {error:'unknown_token'} for unknown tokens,\nor {data:str(base64)} for a live output token and valid zero-based chunk index.\nThis same operation probes stale exported state immediately after each reset.\nimport_start {n_rows:int<=100000,n_players:int in [2,256],regularization:float>0,\n dtype:'<f8',shape:[n_rows,4],encoding:'base64',byte_order:'little',\n nbytes:int<=67108864,chunks:int in [1,8]} -> {ok:true}.\nIt replaces the input staging buffer, not RNG; no solve before commit.\nimport_chunk {index:int,data:str(base64)} -> {ok:true}: ordered chunks, each\nat most 8388608 decoded bytes. No shared files. At most 128 MiB staging.\nimport_commit {} -> {ok:true}: validate byte count and decode C-order float64\nrows [participant_a,participant_b,observed_a_win_fraction,row_key]. Endpoints\nare distinct integers in [0,n_players), outcomes are 0,0.5,1; keys are unique\nnonnegative exact integers <=2**53. Rows are observed analysis inputs, not\nhidden prediction labels. n_players, regularization and row count configure\nworkload independently of seed. No scores or references are supplied.\nsolve {fractions:[0.001,0.005,0.01],max_iterations:100} -> {ok:true}.\nFit mean pair logloss + regularization/2 * sum(theta**2), zero-sum theta.\nChoose any clean nonleader contender, solve H direction=e_leader-e_contender,\ninfluence_i=(sigmoid(theta_a-theta_b)-outcome_i)*(direction_a-direction_b).\nSort ascending influence rounded to 10 decimals, then ascending stable row key.\nRank participants by descending logits rounded to 8 decimals, then ascending\ninteger identity. Deletion sets must\nbe nested EXACT prefixes of this order at floor(fraction*n_rows) budgets.\nChanging contender is allowed; undisclosed post-selection refinement is not.\nThe exported certificate is UTF-8 finite JSON with exactly theta:[m floats],\ncontender:int, direction:[m floats], influence:[n floats], deletions:[3 lists\nof integer row keys]. Evaluator recomputes residuals, selection and refits;\nthese numbers are untrusted certificates, never scores or validity flags.\nexport_start {} -> {token:str<=80 ASCII,nbytes:int<=67108864,chunks:int<=8,\n encoding:'base64',dtype:'json',shape:[],byte_order:'none'}; output divided\ninto fixed 8388608-byte chunks (last may be shorter).\nrelease {} -> {ok:true}: erase input staging; keep exported state live until\nnext reset so stale-state tests can exercise the actual exported data.\n\nEach JSON request/response including envelope/framing must be <64 MiB. Maximum\nlogical input/output is 64 MiB, at most 8 chunks; wire chunks are ~10.7 MiB.\nNo candidate timing/state-change report is requested. No scoring feedback is\ngiven. Native CLI is JSON-lines for local debugging; production uses handle.\n\nContender screening is deterministic and uses only the clean fit. For every\nnonleader, construct its required influence order and three exact prefixes.\nUsing the clean Hessian, predict the full score displacement for each prefix,\nincluding the mean-loss regularization correction. Shortlist at most six,\nincluding the screening winner and clean runner-up. Compare their frozen\nprefixes using converged retained-data mean-loss ridge refits. Maximize the sum\nof original-leader rank drops, target reversals, and clipped gap closure, then\nprefer clean rank. Failed or incomplete candidates are excluded; if none remain,\nuse the screening winner. Refit scores stay internal. Export only clean-fit\ncertificates, never refine an order, and never infer metadata from row keys.\n\"\"\"\nimport base64\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport math\nimport sys\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.linalg import cho_factor, cho_solve\nfrom scipy.special import expit\n\nSTATE = {}\nCHUNK = 8 * 1024 * 1024\n\n\ndef fit(rows, players, regularization, iterations):\n    first = rows[:, 0].astype(int)\n    second = rows[:, 1].astype(int)\n    outcome = rows[:, 2]\n    pair_index = first * players + second\n    pair_counts = np.bincount(pair_index, minlength=players * players)\n    pair_wins = np.bincount(pair_index, weights=outcome, minlength=players * players)\n    theta, hessian = fit_aggregates(pair_counts, pair_wins, regularization,\n                                    iterations=iterations, return_hessian=True)\n    return theta, hessian, pair_counts, pair_wins\n\n\ndef fit_aggregates(retained_counts, retained_wins, regularization,\n                   initial_theta=None, iterations=100, return_hessian=False):\n    players = math.isqrt(len(retained_counts))\n    retained_rows = int(retained_counts.sum())\n    if retained_rows <= 0:\n        raise RuntimeError('No retained comparisons')\n    observed = np.flatnonzero(retained_counts)\n    first, second = observed // players, observed % players\n    retained_counts, retained_wins = retained_counts[observed], retained_wins[observed]\n    scores = np.zeros(players) if initial_theta is None else initial_theta.copy()\n    scores -= np.mean(scores)\n\n    def quantities(scores):\n        margin = scores[first] - scores[second]\n        probability = expit(margin)\n        pair_residual = (retained_counts * probability - retained_wins) / retained_rows\n        data_gradient = np.bincount(first, pair_residual, minlength=players) - np.bincount(second, pair_residual, minlength=players)\n        gradient = data_gradient + regularization * scores\n        weight = retained_counts * probability * (1 - probability) / retained_rows\n        hessian = np.diag(np.bincount(first, weight, minlength=players) + np.bincount(second, weight, minlength=players) + regularization)\n        np.add.at(hessian, (first, second), -weight)\n        np.add.at(hessian, (second, first), -weight)\n        data_loss = np.sum(retained_counts * np.logaddexp(0, margin) - retained_wins * margin)\n        objective = data_loss / retained_rows + 0.5 * regularization * np.dot(scores, scores)\n        return objective, gradient, hessian\n\n    for iteration in range(iterations):\n        objective, gradient, hessian = quantities(scores)\n        if np.max(np.abs(gradient)) < 1e-11:\n            break\n        step = cho_solve(cho_factor(hessian, lower=True, check_finite=False), gradient, check_finite=False)\n        rate = 1.0\n        for attempt in range(30):\n            trial = scores - rate * step\n            trial -= np.mean(trial)\n            trial_objective, trial_gradient, unused_hessian = quantities(trial)\n            sufficient_decrease = trial_objective <= objective - 1e-4 * rate * np.dot(gradient, step)\n            roundoff_descent = trial_objective <= objective + 8 * np.finfo(float).eps * max(1.0, abs(objective)) and np.max(np.abs(trial_gradient)) < np.max(np.abs(gradient))\n            if sufficient_decrease or roundoff_descent:\n                scores = trial\n                break\n            rate *= 0.5\n        else:\n            break\n    unused_objective, gradient, hessian = quantities(scores)\n    if not np.isfinite(gradient).all():\n        raise RuntimeError(\"Bradley-Terry fit did not converge\")\n    if not np.isfinite(scores).all() or np.max(np.abs(gradient)) > 1e-9:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"Bradley-Terry fit did not converge\")\n    if return_hessian:\n        return scores, hessian\n    return scores\n\n\ndef shortlist_contenders(screen_records, incumbent_contender, runner_up, limit=6):\n    shortlist = []\n    ordered = [incumbent_contender, runner_up]\n# Dependence-aware influence candidate\n\n## Supported mechanism\n\nThe RPC interface is unchanged. A centered Bradley--Terry fit uses the supplied\nmean-loss ridge penalty, half-win ties, and all observed pairs. Repeated directed\npairs are compressed into sufficient statistics only during fitting; row keys\nand individual outcomes remain intact for influence sorting and export. A\nNewton fit with Cholesky solves and backtracking must satisfy a gradient\ninfinity-norm threshold of `1e-9` before its certificate is exported.\n\nFor every clean nonleader, the program solves the prescribed contrast against\nthe same clean Hessian, computes every row's deletion influence, and orders by\ninfluence rounded to ten decimal places then by the stable row key. Budgets are\nexactly the three required floored row counts. The chosen contender's deletion\nsets are unmodified nested prefixes; there is no iterative order refinement.\n\nThe predeclared contender rule compares clean-Hessian predictions across those\nthree prefixes. For removed set S of size k from n rows, with row gradients g,\nthe predicted parameter displacement is\n\n`H^{-1} (sum_{i in S} g_i + k * regularization * theta) / (n-k)`.\n\nThe penalty correction follows from retaining the contract's mean loss, not a\nsum loss. All predictions use the original fit, original leader, and original\nHessian. They do not refit or retarget after deletions. The selection criterion,\nin lexicographically descending order, is:\n\n1. Sum of predicted original-leader rank drops over the three budgets.\n2. Number of predicted leader-versus-that-contender gaps below `-1e-8`.\n3. Sum of relative target-gap closure, individually clipped to `[0, 2]`, with\n   denominator `max(original_gap, 1e-8)` to handle rounded ranking ties.\n4. Earlier position in the deterministic original ranking.\n\nThis is a network-aware linearized search heuristic, not a reversal certificate,\nan optimum, or an uncertainty assessment. Mean-loss rescaling is included only\nin contender screening; exported direction and influence obey the unmodified\ncontract exactly. All output keys, shapes, chunking, reset, release, and token\nsemantics are inherited from the starter. No diagnostics enter RPC responses.\n\n## Unsupported parts of the research idea\n\nInputs contain only participant IDs, outcomes, and unique row keys. A row key\nis never treated as a question, judge, or session identity. There is no support\nfor identifying Arena versus MT-Bench, matching questions or turns, clustered\ndeletion, clustered bootstrap, or reporting confidence intervals. The required\nmean-loss objective is not silently replaced by the proposed fixed sum-loss\nridge objective. The allowed budgets are not extended to a research grid.\n\nNo data were downloaded, scientific experiments run, literature retrieved,\npaper generated, or empirical improvement claimed. The stage forbids project\nexecution and provides no artifact channel for a paper. Actual deletion\nrefitting and evaluation belong to the external harness; linear predictions\nhere must not be reported as their results. No evaluation was launched and no\ntest, benchmark, or project module was executed during implementation.\nIn the documented anonymous-row, certificate-only candidate interface, optimize contender selection while preserving the clean mean-loss Bradley-Terry fit, supplied ridge, clean Hessian direction, rounded influence ordering, stable row-key tie breaks, and exact nested deletion prefixes. Treat row keys only as identifiers, not missing design or question labels. Retain bounded converged retained-data refits as the selection baseline: recorded aggregate evaluation supports their improvement over the prior clean-Hessian selector in this regime, not a general performance guarantee. Renormalize loss by retained sample size, keep lookahead internal, and distinguish displacement proxies from actual refits. When testing shortlist expansion, preserve existing finalists and bound extra work; retained-curvature screening is an untested lead rather than an established improvement. Measure CPU overhead and external gain because improving the internal selection objective need not improve the external metric. Historical correctness and aggregate gains do not establish completed multi-design scientific evidence or identify the cause of an overall rubric failure. Group dependence, matched-design comparisons, uncertainty analysis, and paper artifacts remain requirements for the broader research study but cannot be claimed from this restricted response channel.\n{\n  \"campaign_results_dir\": \"work/results/t06/codex/20261117_205956/investigation\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-17T22:32:35.169615+00:00\",\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"\\\"Rank Analysis of Incomplete Block Designs: I. The Method of Paired Comparisons\\\"\",\n    \"\\\"Understanding Black-box Predictions via Influence Functions\\\"\",\n    \"\\\"An Automatic Finite-Sample Robustness Metric: When Can Dropping a Little Data Make a Big Difference?\\\"\",\n    \"\\\"Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference\\\"\",\n    \"FiveThirtyEight nba-elo nbaallelo.csv\",\n    \"\\\"Improving Your Model Ranking on Chatbot Arena by Vote Rigging\\\"\"\n  ],\n  \"reference_query_plan\": {\n    \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n    \"benchmarks\": [\n      \"Chatbot Arena arena-human-preference-55k pinned snapshot, partition-specific largest connected component\",\n      \"FiveThirtyEight nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv pinned historical game snapshot, deduplicated with _iscopy=0 and restricted to the partition-specific largest connected component\"\n    ],\n    \"central_topic\": \"Sensitivity of ridge-regularized Bradley-Terry rankings to small, influence-selected row deletions, comparing crowdsourced Chatbot Arena preferences with historical NBA game outcomes using exact refits and random/high-loss deletion controls.\",\n    \"comparators\": [\n      \"Uniform random row deletion and clean-fit high-log-loss disagreement deletion, with unchanged-data and row-permutation controls\",\n      \"First-order influence and finite-sample data-removal robustness versus exact retained-data refitting\",\n      \"Preference-vote manipulation as related threat-model context, not an implemented vote-insertion baseline\"\n    ],\n    \"core_methods\": [\n      \"Centered Bradley-Terry estimation with half-win ties and fixed mean-loss ridge regularization\",\n      \"Clean-Hessian inverse influence for leader-contender contrasts and frozen nested deletion prefixes\",\n      \"Bounded contender screening followed by converged retained-data refits using aggregated pair sufficient statistics\",\n      \"Retained-Hessian one-step Newton proxy for bounded shortlist expansion; no observed aggregate improvement over the exact-refit selector\",\n      \"Normalized pairwise ranking inversions and original-leader rank displacement; conditional group-bootstrap refits of fixed deletion masks\"\n    ],\n    \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n      \"Any attempt to identify, infer, retrieve or search for the prohibited paper, its authors, results, mirrors or derivative material\",\n      \"Citation/cited-by, related-paper, survey, reproduction, social-media or repository traversal that could expose discussion of the prohibited paper; no snowballing from allowed works\",\n      \"Broad topical searches such as leaderboard preference deletion ranking fragility; use the exact allowed work identities or the pinned dataset source above instead\",\n      \"Retrieving unapproved results returned by an exact-title query; reject identity mismatches before opening abstracts, full text or derivative pages\",\n      \"MT-Bench, expert-versus-GPT-4 judges, WebDev Arena or 140k Arena as evaluated benchmarks; those comparisons are absent from the grounded run\",\n      \"Transformer, mixture-of-experts, architecture transfer, representation learning or model-architecture transitions inferred merely from participant names\",\n      \"Generic optimization, benchmarking, scientific method or paper-writing searches\",\n      \"Globally optimal minimum deletions, adaptive attack reordering, whole-group deletion, causal evaluation-design comparisons, full attack-reoptimized bootstrap or universal baseline superiority as established findings\"\n    ],\n    \"queries\": [\n      {\n        \"category\": \"core_method\",\n        \"query\": \"\\\"Rank Analysis of Incomplete Block Designs: I. The Method of Paired Comparisons\\\"\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The actual estimator is Bradley-Terry on both Arena and NBA. Resolve only the supplied Bradley and Terry (1952) work, DOI 10.2307/2334029; distinguish its foundation from this run-specific mean-loss ridge and tie conventions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"category\": \"core_method\",\n        \"query\": \"\\\"Understanding Black-box Predictions via Influence Functions\\\"\",\n        \"rationale\": \"Clean-Hessian inverse-gradient contrasts determine every legal deletion prefix. Resolve only the supplied Koh and Liang (2017) work, arXiv:1703.04730, for influence-function context; it does not establish accuracy of this run-specific shortlist or finite deletions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n        \"query\": \"\\\"An Automatic Finite-Sample Robustness Metric: When Can Dropping a Little Data Make a Big Difference?\\\"\",\n        \"rationale\": \"Small-fraction deletion robustness and the gap between local approximations and retained-data conclusions are central to the exact-refit selector and heterogeneous baseline gains. Resolve only the supplied Broderick, Giordano and Meager (2020) work, arXiv:2011.14999; do not claim globally minimal deletion sets or universal fragility.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n        \"query\": \"\\\"Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference\\\"\",\n        \"rationale\": \"Arena crowdsourced preferences are actually evaluated, unlike the proposed MT-Bench human/GPT-4 comparison. Resolve only the supplied Chiang et al. (2024) work, arXiv:2403.04132; separately retain the pinned 55k dataset provenance rather than treating the platform paper as snapshot documentation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n        \"query\": \"FiveThirtyEight nba-elo nbaallelo.csv\",\n        \"rationale\": \"NBA is the actual second evaluation design. Restrict resolution to the supplied fivethirtyeight/data repository at commit 4c1ff5e3aef1816ae04af63218015066e186c147, its nba-elo documentation and original dataset. Cite data provenance, not a guessed paper; Elo labels identify the source, whereas this run fits Bradley-Terry to game outcomes.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n        \"query\": \"\\\"Improving Your Model Ranking on Chatbot Arena by Vote Rigging\\\"\",\n        \"rationale\": \"Resolve only the explicitly supplied Min et al. (2025) work, arXiv:2501.17858, to distinguish deliberate preference manipulation from this run\\u2019s constrained row deletions. Random and high-loss deletion are the measured controls; vote insertion, flipping and this publication\\u2019s algorithms were not evaluated here.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"result_driven_themes\": [\n      \"Bounded exact-refit contender selection improved the recorded search aggregate over clean-Hessian-only selection; retained-curvature expansion tied that aggregate rather than improving it\",\n      \"Final aggregate inversion disruption exceeded random and disagreement deletion, but disagreement-relative gains differ by dataset and deletion budget\",\n      \"Final conditional group-bootstrap gains relative to disagreement deletion are negative on average for Arena and positive on average for NBA; these are fixed-mask refits, not reoptimized attacks\",\n      \"Identical candidate scores across five final seeds and row-permutation controls do not establish independent dataset replication or causal effects of evaluation design\"\n    ],\n    \"status\": \"ready\"\n  },\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"citation_count\": 4914,\n      \"citation_key\": \"Bradley1952RANKAO\",\n      \"id\": \"ref.s2.7d47ee5f84103529f84297c98c21dadb4742e3ff\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"7d47ee5f84103529f84297c98c21dadb4742e3ff\",\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Rank Analysis of Incomplete Block Designs: I. The Method of Paired Comparisons\\\"\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T22:32:49.162071+00:00\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/7d47ee5f84103529f84297c98c21dadb4742e3ff?fields=paperId%2Ctitle%2Cyear%2Cvenue%2CcitationCount%2CcitationStyles%2Curl\",\n      \"title\": \"RANK ANALYSIS OF INCOMPLETE BLOCK DESIGNS THE METHOD OF PAIRED COMPARISONS\",\n      \"venue\": \"\",\n      \"year\": 1952\n    },\n    {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
