{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport difflib,json,subprocess\nstrategy = {\n 'status':'ready',\n 'selected_prompt_family':'paper_standalone',\n 'recommended_title':'Exact-Refit Audits of Pairwise Ranking Sensitivity',\n 'central_question':'How do small, influence-ordered deletions affect regularized Bradley–Terry rankings, and how does their disruption compare with random and high-loss deletion across crowd preferences and historical sports outcomes?',\n 'central_thesis':'A frozen-prefix audit with bounded exact-refit contender selection yields greater aggregate rank disruption than the measured deletion baselines on the final partitions, but its advantage depends on dataset and deletion budget, with fixed-mask group resampling favoring high-loss deletion in Arena and the studied selector in NBA.',\n 'narrative_rationale':'The strongest evidence is a direct final comparison on two real-data settings plus a clearly delimited conditional uncertainty analysis, not an optimization success story. The shared object is sensitivity of the fitted ranking under row removal; Arena and NBA are condition-specific evidence blocks under that common question, not matched causal tests of evaluation design. Mechanism comes from inspected implementations; empirical effectiveness comes from independent evaluator refits. Search configurations provide descriptive supporting context only. Neither broad leaderboard fragility nor universal method superiority follows.',\n 'prior_paper_story_status':'recentered',\n 'edit_framing_decision':'Fresh framing, not an edit pass: no previous_paper directory, resolved edit-context files, aggregated directory, extra_tasks directory, or prior manuscript was staged. Recenter the broad source-task motivation and unexecuted grouped-deletion proposal on the measured anonymous-row, frozen-prefix audit. Compare three candidate framings: preserving a dependence-aware, multi-judge frontier story is unsupported; broadening to a universal cross-design robustness method overstates unmatched evidence; centering the shared row-deletion mechanism and its heterogeneous measured limits is supported. This status describes that source-brief recentering, not replacement of an unseen manuscript.',\n 'staged_input_impact':[\n  'paper_data.json has no downstream_study and no study.* grounding IDs. Read final_evaluation independently: final.qualification.status is completed, final.qualification.outcome is qualified, and final.qualification.correct is true. Qualification is not evidence of generalization or superiority; absent additional gate diagnostics remain unknown.',\n  'The inspected best_program_manifest.json identifies the six-contender exact-refit configuration. Its source matches the named method family; final provenance includes a program-tree digest, but no independent reconstruction of that tree digest is claimed.',\n  'Final episode cases contain Arena and NBA, not MT-Bench expert/GPT-4 comparisons. Dependence groups exist for partitioning and conditional resampling, not as deletion units or labels available to the selector.',\n  'Final results and repeated fixed-mask bootstrap records change the story from a general improvement claim to dataset- and budget-dependent sensitivity. Reversed-row cases are controls, not new datasets; repeated bootstrap exports are not independent replicate sets.',\n  'Clean-Hessian-only selection and retained-curvature shortlist expansion are descriptive search configurations, not fully replicated final ablations. No available grounded metric for the original starter comparator supports retaining its quantitative comparison.',\n  'The staged bibliography includes irrelevant retrieval matches and lacks several exact planned identities. It supports neither a completed literature review nor a novelty claim; later literature verification must remain within the allowed identities and source restrictions.'\n ],\n 'method_groups':[\n  {'name':'Frozen-prefix bounded exact-refit selector','role':'proposed','include_reason':'The studied six-contender configuration: clean Bradley–Terry fit, frozen clean-Hessian influence orders, bounded retained-data refits, then independent evaluation of the selected prefixes. It is the only method with the final proposed-method evidence.'},\n  {'name':'Uniform random row deletion','role':'baseline','include_reason':'Measured at identical floored row budgets; five final seeds quantify randomness in this deletion rule, not independent sampling of datasets.'},\n  {'name':'High-loss row deletion','role':'baseline','include_reason':'Measured strong comparator, ordered by clean per-row Bradley–Terry negative log-likelihood; this is not disagreement between judges. Its Arena advantage is essential to the thesis.'},\n  {'name':'Unmodified-data refit and row-order controls','role':'supporting','include_reason':'Document numerical zero-change and row-order invariance checks without presenting internal pass rates as performance metrics or counting controls as additional data.'},\n  {'name':'Clean-Hessian-only contender selection','role':'ablation','include_reason':'Supporting descriptive search comparison for the role of retained-data lookahead, with raw recorded configuration scores only; no final causal attribution or search-adjusted uncertainty is available.'},\n  {'name':'Retained-curvature shortlist expansion','role':'ablation','include_reason':'Supporting negative configuration comparison: a larger proxy pool and exact shortlist have the same recorded search aggregate as the studied selector. This does not establish identical masks, equivalence, or an efficiency result.'}\n ],\n 'results_focus':[\n  'Lead with final dataset-by-budget normalized inversion fractions for the studied selector, high-loss deletion, and random deletion. Use unpermuted cases only for performance summaries; paired reversed-row cases remain controls.',\n  'Retain final aggregate values as a compact secondary table: studied selector 0.011722185737596697, high-loss deletion 0.009340217174121283, random deletion 0.003902759168170127; paired gains 0.002381968563475412 and 0.00781942656942657 respectively. Ground in final.metric.* IDs, not search scores.',\n  'Show every stored conditional group-bootstrap gain for each dataset and comparator. There are 32 stored records per dataset, repeated identically across exported final episodes, not five independent sets. All stored high-loss-relative gains are negative in Arena and positive in NBA; random-relative Arena gains include negative values.',\n  'Distinguish seed-resampling intervals from group-resampling displays. The aggregate high-loss-relative interval has equal endpoints because that contrast is deterministic across the recorded seeds; it does not quantify dataset uncertainty or establish certain superiority.',\n  'Use normalized original-leader rank drop 0.03305066318764949 only as a secondary aggregate for the studied method. The stored per-budget curves measure normalized inversions, not leader rank drop or minimum rank-reversal budgets.',\n  'Place a compact descriptive search-configuration table after the main final comparisons, explicitly separate from final evidence: raw scores 0.013240276724865765 for clean-Hessian-only selection and 0.01595532053579999 for each exact-refit configuration. Do not show search trajectories, derived campaign score deltas, winner claims, or scored/unscored attempt counts because study.* sources are absent.'\n ],\n 'figure_plan':[\n  'Figure 1, early overview: six-block mechanism/evidence map, showing two data settings feeding a shared fitted-ranking deletion audit; annotate frozen orders, bounded refits, and conditional fixed-mask resampling.',\n  'Figure 2, main comparison: two dataset panels of grouped dots at the three measured deletion budgets, comparing normalized inversions for exact-refit selection, high-loss deletion, and random deletion. Budget is a condition, not an optimization trajectory.',\n  'Figure 3, uncertainty and limitations: two comparator panels showing the unique stored conditional bootstrap gains for Arena and NBA against a zero line. Keep masks fixed and do not label the distributions as full-procedure bootstrap confidence.'\n ],\n 'figure_specs':[\n  {\n   'figure_id':'audit_overview','required':True,'purpose':'Make the common sensitivity object, selection mechanism, independent refits, and conditional uncertainty scope legible before the results.',\n   'figure_type':'diagram','preferred_generator':'generate-concept-figure','group_by':'audit_component','metric':'none; conceptual mechanism only','split_by':'Arena and NBA input badges within one shared mechanism',\n   'preferred_span':'full_width','target_width_fraction':0.98,\n   'layout_rationale':'paper.sty specifies a single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height. At 0.98 text width the diagram is about 5.39 inches wide; a two-row, three-block layout gives six short blocks without tiny text. A half-width placement would obscure frozen-prefix versus refit distinctions.',\n   'allow_progression':False,'max_panels':1,'max_panels_per_row':1,'max_categories_per_panel':6,'max_series_per_panel':3,\n   'placement':'Page 1 or 2, before the detailed method; ordinary single-column figure spanning the text block.',\n   'intended_paper_footprint':'About 0.65 page including caption; artwork about 2.6 inches tall.',\n   'min_rendered_font_pt':9,\n   'content_spec':'Six blocks: observed comparisons (Arena crowd preferences / NBA game outcomes); regularized Bradley–Terry clean fit; frozen influence-prefix candidates; bounded exact-refit contender selection; deletion and independent retained-data refits; dataset/budget comparisons. Attach a short conditional-resampling note to the final block: reuse masks, refit weights, do not reselect. Do not depict grouped deletion, causal design effects, adaptive row reordering, or an architecture change.',\n   'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes'],\n   'implementation_sources':['results/ideas/02_exact_refit_contender_screening/program_1/src/program.py','task/evaluate_final.py']\n  },\n  {\n   'figure_id':'dataset_budget_comparison','required':True,'purpose':'Expose the dataset and deletion-budget contrasts hidden by the aggregate score.',\n   'figure_type':'grouped_dot','preferred_generator':'matplotlib','group_by':'deletion_rule','metric':'normalized pairwise inversion fraction relative to the clean fitted ranking','split_by':'dataset',\n   'preferred_span':'full_width','target_width_fraction':1.0,\n   'layout_rationale':'Use the full 5.5-inch text width for two side-by-side panels, each with three budget groups and three methods. Keep common axes and one shared legend, with no long method labels inside panels. A half-text-width entire figure would shrink each panel below readable size.',\n   'allow_progression':False,'max_panels':2,'max_panels_per_row':2,'max_categories_per_panel':3,'max_series_per_panel':3,\n   'placement':'Main results, approximately page 5 or 6.',\n   'intended_paper_footprint':'About 0.70 page including caption; artwork about 3.0 inches tall.',\n   'min_rendered_font_pt':9,\n   'content_spec':'Use 0.1%, 0.5%, and 1% nominal deleted-row fractions, with floor-count convention in the caption. Plot studied selector and high-loss deletion as deterministic markers; show the five random-deletion seed values as faint same-color points plus their mean. Do not add confidence bars to deterministic repeated values or pool reversed-row controls as extra replicates. Use a common y scale and label larger values as greater disruption, not better ranking quality.',\n   'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes'],\n   'implementation_sources':['task/evaluate_final.py:FRACTIONS','task/evaluate_final.py:disruption']\n  },\n  {\n   'figure_id':'conditional_resampling_contrasts','required':True,'purpose':'Make the dependence-sensitive, fixed-mask limitations and dataset-specific comparator ordering visible in the main paper.',\n   'figure_type':'strip_plot','preferred_generator':'matplotlib','group_by':'dataset','metric':'conditional bootstrap difference in mean normalized inversion fraction: studied selector minus comparator','split_by':'comparator: high-loss deletion and random deletion',\n   'preferred_span':'full_width','target_width_fraction':1.0,\n   'layout_rationale':'Two panels over the full 5.5-inch text width leave space for 32 observations per dataset, a zero reference, and explicit comparator titles at 9-point or larger. Use deterministic display jitter, not additional statistical sampling; no third panel or dense legends.',\n   'allow_progression':False,'max_panels':2,'max_panels_per_row':2,'max_categories_per_panel':2,'max_series_per_panel':1,\n   'placement':'Immediately after the main comparison, approximately page 6 or 7; never only in the supplement.',\n   'intended_paper_footprint':'About 0.65 page including caption; artwork about 2.7 inches tall.',\n   'min_rendered_font_pt':9,\n   'content_spec':'Extract the one unique conditional_group_bootstrap block, plot its 32 stored gains per dataset per comparator, and mark zero. Label as fixed-mask, group-weight resampling. Do not pool repeated exports into 160 records; do not claim reoptimized attacks, population significance, independent dataset replication, or a causal evaluation-design effect.',\n   'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes'],\n   'implementation_sources':['task/evaluate_final.py:final_uncertainty']\n  }\n ],\n 'supplementary_plan':{\n  'purpose':'Provide implementation, data-provenance, numerical-control, and uncertainty detail needed to reproduce and audit the narrow claim; keep the dataset/budget contrasts, opposite conditional gain signs, and their limitations in the main paper.',\n  'planned_subsections':[\n   {'heading':'Retained-mean objective and frozen-prefix selection','grounded_content':'Expand the ridge Bradley–Terry objective, centered parameterization, half-win ties, clean contrast solves, mean-loss displacement correction, deterministic tie rules, six-contender selection, warm starts, pair-statistic compression, convergence checks, and incomplete-refit fallback. Explain why selector rank-drop objectives and evaluation inversion fractions differ. The source explains algorithms, not evidence of individual internal shortlist decisions. Supporting files: the studied program.py and IMPLEMENTATION.md; task/evaluate_final.py.', 'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes']},\n   {'heading':'Data partitioning and reproducibility record','grounded_content':'Document the pinned Arena and NBA sources from the task brief and staged manifests; content-group split, partition-specific largest connected component, NBA _iscopy filtering, heterogeneous historical team codes, exact source/payload hashes, row keys, declared seeds, and CPU-only dependencies. Reproduce recorded results from staged evaluation resources. Clearly distinguish inspected protocol metadata from numerical findings in paper_data.json; do not claim an available raw-to-partition builder, complete raw-data archive, or measured runtime scaling when none is staged.', 'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes','final.qualification.status']},\n   {'heading':'Numerical and row-order checks','grounded_content':'Document clean-fit and contrast residual tolerances, no-deletion zero disruption, rejection of invalid constant certificates, and reversed-row invariance. Explain that numerical certificate checks are narrower than scientific generalization, and that candidate-authored diagnostics are explicitly unverified. Preserve the reported final outcome using qualification sources, without inferring undocumented gate causes.', 'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes','final.qualification.status','final.qualification.outcome','final.qualification.correct']},\n   {'heading':'Descriptive search configurations','grounded_content':'Archive a full raw-score table for clean-Hessian selection, bounded exact refits, and retained-curvature expansion, tied to their inspected implementations. State the missing controlled final ablations and selection-adjusted uncertainty. The two exact-refit configurations tie the recorded search aggregate; do not infer identical behavior or negligible overhead. No campaign count, unscored-loss, search-delta, or original-starter comparison is supported through study.* IDs.', 'source_ids':['result.top_program_01','result.top_program_02','result.top_program_03']},\n   {'heading':'Conditional uncertainty protocol and stored records','grounded_content':'Give the group-weight bootstrap algorithm and the complete unique stored records. Contrast it with seed-resampling intervals: fixed masks are not reselected, and the nominal row fraction does not fix removed resampled weight. Explain the small resample count and repeated exports, and why zero-width deterministic seed intervals are not population certainty. Main results retain the essential signed contrasts.', 'source_ids':['final.evidence.episodes','final.metric.paired_gain_disagreement_ci_low','final.metric.paired_gain_disagreement_ci_high','final.metric.paired_gain_random_ci_low','final.metric.paired_gain_random_ci_high']},\n   {'heading':'LLM Usage','grounded_content':'Disclose only the roles evidenced by the supplied agent-run logs and generated artifacts: research ideation, candidate code generation, reference-query planning, and manuscript assistance. Distinguish these activities from the numerical evaluator; do not invent provider/model identities, undisclosed human review, or paid API experiments. This is process provenance, not a scientific metric claim.', 'source_ids':[]}\n  ]\n },\n 'forbidden_headings':['Search Progression','Campaign Phases','Milestones','First Valid Result','Timeline of Improvements','Experiment A','Experiment B','From Failure to Success','Winning Configuration'],\n 'forbidden_phrases':['we then tried','first valid result','subsequent improvements','next-best','later variants','search winner','universally more robust','universally superior','state of the art','globally minimal deletion budget','causal effect of evaluation design','full-procedure bootstrap','five independent datasets','160 independent bootstrap replicates','correctness rate','correct and eligible','eligible','combined_score','rubric','worker_01','worker_02'],\n 'notes_for_writer':[\n  'Deliver only a thesis-first standalone paper later. This strategy stage writes neither paper.tex nor claims_grounding.json and does not invoke final paper validation. Treat all dotted JSON field names, hashes, internal selection metadata, and pipeline terminology in this memo as private-only grounding, not manuscript labels.',\n  'Target the supplied ICLR 2026 single-column template and VENUE.md, not a two-column conference assumption. paper.sty sets textwidth=5.5 inches and textheight=9 inches; column width equals full text width. All required figures use that one text block, not a two-column figure* layout. Do not change typography or margins to meet the page constraint.',\n  'Budget approximately 8.5 body pages: title/abstract 0.5; introduction 1.0; related work 0.6; method 1.6; data/metrics/uncertainty protocol 1.1; results 2.6; discussion/limitations 0.8; conclusion 0.3. These are page-equivalent allocations including figures and tables, not measured final pagination. Target 8–9 body pages excluding references; an 8-page body needs its final page at least 75% full. Allocate figure 1 to the introduction/method, and figures 2–3 to results. Use space for objective derivation, comparator definitions, budget-wise contrasts, resampling scope, and failure analysis, not additional unsupported experiments.',\n  'Use headings Introduction; Related Work; Frozen-Prefix Ranking Audits; Data, Metrics, and Uncertainty; Dataset- and Budget-Dependent Disruption; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Supplementary Information follows references in the same source, with a separate LLM Usage subsection. Organize Arena and NBA around one common question with condition-specific limitations, not discovery order.',\n  'Use three required figures and two compact tables: final aggregate metrics with seed-interval interpretation, and descriptive search configurations. The search table can be short in the main body with full implementation detail after references; never promote it to an independently replicated mechanism attribution. Additional grounded detail may fill the body only when it clarifies the thesis.',\n  'Private source map: primary evidence final.evidence.episodes; aggregate inversions final.metric.combined_score; comparator metrics final.metric.random_baseline_score and final.metric.disagreement_baseline_score; gains final.metric.paired_gain_vs_random and final.metric.paired_gain_vs_disagreement; secondary metric final.metric.mean_leader_rank_drop; qualification facts only final.qualification.*. Do not use memory as quantitative evidence.',\n  'Reader-facing metrics: normalized pairwise inversion fraction = discordant participant pairs divided by all participant pairs; normalized original-leader rank drop = its downward position change divided by participant count minus one. These are different statistics. Deletion size is removed rows divided by original rows, with floored counts; equal fractions do not equalize effective sample size, exposure, leverage, graph structure, or resampled weight across datasets.',\n  'Protocol from inspected code: mean negative log-likelihood plus ridge with lambda=0.001; ties as half wins; budgets 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%; all candidate prefix orders computed on the original clean fit. Bounded selection uses actual retained-data refits, but this is not adaptive reordering and not globally optimal subset search. Refit means numerical optimization to stated residual tolerances, not symbolic exactness.',\n  'Do not derive a group-deletion frontier from these artifacts. Row deletions are anonymous to the selector; provenance groups support splitting and conditional resampling only. No matched-support, expert/judge comparison, bootstrap reselection, ridge-sensitivity experiment, or runtime ablation has been measured here.',\n  'Final aggregate high-loss-relative interval is [0.002381968563475412, 0.002381968563475412]; random-relative interval is [0.007377907506332164, 0.008168732424939618]. Explain their seed-resampling construction in the main table caption and distinguish them from conditional group resampling. Do not turn the first interval into a generalization statement.',\n  'Current final qualification is completed and qualified, not a failed evaluation. Report exactly that if needed in reproducibility/audit text, without interpreting qualification as statistical superiority. If later authoritative evidence reports failed qualification, preserve the failure in writing, review, audit, and revision; distinguish statistical/performance causes from validity/adaptivity causes only when diagnostics identify them. Missing causes remain unknown.',\n  'No downstream_study or study.* IDs exist. Do not reconstruct campaign selection claims, scored/unscored attempt counts, search score differences, or original-starter baseline performance from private memory. Descriptive raw search configuration values have result.top_program_* sources; final results have separate final.* sources. Missing performance evidence is not a measured loss.',\n  'Contribution claims should be an explicit auditable protocol, measured two-setting budget contrasts, and a conditional uncertainty analysis with limitations. Do not claim first-of-kind novelty, a new Bradley–Terry estimator, optimal attack certificates, causal evaluation-design findings, or superiority across public leaderboards.',\n  'The bibliography is not yet an adequate checked related-work set: exact planned influence-function, finite-sample robustness, Arena, vote-rigging, and NBA provenance identities were not all resolved in the staged retrieval. Use only exact allowed identities after verifying both bibliographic records and actually consulted content; existing irrelevant matches are not citation permission. No broad leaderboard searches, citation traversal, guessing of the prohibited work, or use of unconsulted papers. No network retrieval is required for this strategy.',\n  'Render all quantitative plots reproducibly with source plus vector PDF and 300-DPI PNG. Reference PDF from LaTeX whenever it exists. Request editable draw.io plus PDF/PNG for the concept overview when available; use its raster only if no PDF exists. Raster-only output must be at least 2400 pixels wide for these full-width figures (1200 for any later justified column-scale raster); regenerate rather than upscale. Follow concept-figure orchestration without invoking rendering during this strategy stage.',\n  'Use consistent colors for methods in figure 2 and consistent dataset colors in figure 3, with labeled legends stating those distinct roles. Prefer 9–10-point rendered labels and captions that state sampling units. A zero reference is not a fourth method series. No thresholded success-rate figure is warranted.',\n  'Honor double-blind presentation: omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and self-identifying/internal links; remove the template final-copy/author scaffold at drafting time while preserving required template watermark. Do not manufacture a conference checklist that is not supplied; include the separately required LLM Usage disclosure.',\n  'The raw-to-partition preprocessing builder and all original source archives are not staged as a complete reproducibility package. Describe that limitation explicitly and inventory the available evaluator, candidate code, normalized resources, versions, and hashes; do not claim a fully reconstructed raw-data pipeline. Runtime observations are not comparative CPU-cost evidence.',\n  'Substantive supplementary content is already grounded; do not wait for new experiments or guess posthoc rankings. When authoritative posthoc rankings arrive separately, route appendix-ranked analyses into Supplementary Information without changing this strategy or displacing central evidence.'\n ],\n 'failure_reason':''\n}\nmd = r'''# Paper strategy: exact-refit audits of pairwise ranking sensitivity\n\n## Decision and scope\n**Status: ready.** Final prompt family: \"'`paper_standalone`; writing skill: `write-paper-standalone`. Provisional title: **Exact-Refit Audits of Pairwise Ranking Sensitivity**.\n\n**Central question.** How do small, influence-ordered deletions affect regularized Bradley–Terry rankings, and how does their disruption compare with random and high-loss deletion across crowd preferences and historical sports outcomes?\n\n**Central thesis.** A frozen-prefix audit with bounded exact-refit contender selection yields greater aggregate rank disruption than the measured deletion baselines on the final partitions, but its advantage depends on dataset and deletion budget, with fixed-mask group resampling favoring high-loss deletion in Arena and the studied selector in NBA.\n\nThis is a plan, not a manuscript. Internal JSON field names and provenance identifiers below are private-only grounding instructions, not reader-facing terminology.\n\n## Evidence boundary and qualification\nRead the final evaluation independently of campaign selection. `paper_data.json` contains no `downstream_study` and no `study.*` sources. Its authoritative qualification sources report:\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n\nDo not invent a failed qualification for this run, or interpret the reported qualification as statistical superiority, generalization, or completion of every original deliverable. Missing additional gate diagnostics remain unknown. If authoritative evidence changes to failed qualification, preserve that failure in writing, review, audit, and revision; classify its cause only when evaluator diagnostics support doing so.\n\nPrimary final evidence is `final.evidence.episodes`. Its five seed exports contain Arena and NBA cases, paired reversed-row controls, and conditional group-bootstrap records. Candidate-authored diagnostics are marked unverified; ground effectiveness in evaluator refits, not candidate assertions. The staged evaluator independently computes the per-row loss baseline and retained-data rankings.\n\n`results/best_program_manifest.json` identifies the studied six-contender configuration, whose implementation was inspected directly. Final provenance carries a program-tree digest; no independent reconstruction of that digest is claimed. Method-family linkage is supported, without pretending to have a stronger source-tree audit.\n\nNo grounded metric for the original starter comparator is available through the supplied study sources: omit its quantitative comparison. Do not infer scored/unscored attempt counts, campaign winner claims, or search-score differences from memory. Missing scores are not losses.\n\n## Framing choice, not inherited chronology\nNo resolved edit-context files, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, `extra_tasks/`, or prior manuscript are staged. The required story status is **recentered**: it describes recentering the source-task brief, not editing an unseen paper.\n\nThree framings were compared:\n1. **Preserve a dependence-aware, multi-judge frontier story:** reject. Whole-group deletion, matched support, and expert/GPT-4 comparisons were proposed but are not the measured study.\n2. **Broaden to a universal cross-design robustness method:** reject. The datasets are unmatched; design, participant pool, sample size, comparison graph, and era are confounded. Aggregate advantage is not universal superiority.\n3. **Center a shared row-deletion audit and its heterogeneous limits:** select. The same fitted-ranking object and constrained mechanism are evaluated on two real-data settings, with direct budget comparisons and a clearly bounded uncertainty analysis.\n\nTreat Arena and NBA as condition-specific evidence blocks under one common question. Do not organize them as successive experiments. The scientific contribution is an auditable protocol, measured budget-wise contrasts, and conditional uncertainty with explicit limits—not a new estimator or established first-of-kind attack.\n\n## Method groups\n| Group | Role | Inclusion rule |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Frozen-prefix bounded exact-refit selector | Studied method | Clean fit and influence orders, at most six contenders, converged retained-data selection, independently evaluated prefixes. |\n| Uniform random row deletion | Baseline | Identical floored budgets; five seeds reflect deletion randomness, not five datasets. |\n| High-loss row deletion | Baseline | Descending clean per-row negative log-likelihood; not inter-judge disagreement. Essential strong comparator. |\n| Unmodified-data refit and reversed-row cases | Numerical controls | Zero-change and invariance checks, not performance-rate headlines or independent replications. |\n| Clean-Hessian-only contender selection | Supporting descriptive configuration | Search-partition context for lookahead, not an independently replicated final ablation. |\n| Retained-curvature shortlist expansion | Supporting descriptive configuration | Same recorded search aggregate as bounded exact refits; no equivalence or efficiency inference. |\n\nExplain the mean-loss ridge Bradley–Terry objective, centered scores, half-win ties, clean leader/contender contrast, fixed clean-Hessian influence ordering, retained-mean penalty correction, shortlist selection, and independent evaluation. The inspected evaluator uses ridge `0.001` and deletion fractions `0.001`, `0.005`, and `0.01`; these are protocol settings, not newly measured findings.\n\nThe inspected correction for a prefix of size k among n rows is the clean inverse Hessian applied to the sum of removed row gradients plus k times ridge times clean parameters, divided by n−k. It is an approximation for screening; actual retained-data fits decide the bounded final selection. Explain convergence tolerances, deterministic tie rules, caching, warm starts, and incomplete-refit fallback. “Exact refit” means optimization to stated residual tolerances, not symbolic exactness.\n\nThe selector prioritizes original-leader rank drop; the primary reported evaluation statistic is the normalized pairwise inversion fraction. This mismatch limits interpretation and must not be disguised as direct optimization of the reporting metric. No adaptive row reordering, whole-group deletion, or global minimum-deletion certificate is established.\n\n## Results that carry the story\n**Lead with the final dataset × deletion-budget comparison**, not the aggregate or searched configurations. Keep unpermuted cases for performance and reversed-row cases for invariance only.\n\nDefine two different statistics:\n- Normalized pairwise inversion fraction: discordant participant pairs divided by all participant pairs, relative to the clean fitted ordering.\n- Normalized original-leader rank drop: downward position change of the clean leader divided by participant count minus one.\n\nThe stored per-budget curves are the first statistic. Larger values mean greater disruption, not better ranking accuracy. Row-removal fraction normalizes intervention size, not effective sample size, leverage, exposure, comparison-graph structure, or statistical difficulty.\n\nA compact secondary table may report the following final aggregate values, with sources kept explicit:\n\n| Quantity | Stored value | Grounding ID |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Studied selector inversion fraction | 0.011722185737596697 | `final.metric.combined_score` |\n| High-loss deletion inversion fraction | 0.009340217174121283 | `final.metric.disagreement_baseline_score` |\n| Random deletion inversion fraction | 0.003902759168170127 | `final.metric.random_baseline_score` |\n| Paired gain over high-loss deletion | 0.002381968563475412 | `final.metric.paired_gain_vs_disagreement` |\n| Paired gain over random deletion | 0.00781942656942657 | `final.metric.paired_gain_vs_random` |\n| Studied selector normalized leader rank drop | 0.03305066318764949 | `final.metric.mean_leader_rank_drop` |\n\nUse sensible visible precision, but retain stored precision in analysis outputs. Do not relabel aggregate metrics as dataset-specific results.\n\n**Conditional uncertainty is central, not an appendix escape hatch.** There is one unique block of 32 stored group-bootstrap records per dataset; it is repeated identically across seed exports. Do not count it five times. All stored high-loss-relative gains are negative in Arena and positive in NBA. Arena random-relative gains include negative observations. Plot the actual stored records, not invented intervals or new reoptimized attacks.\n\nThese records resample provenance-group weights, refit clean and perturbed models, and reuse the original masks. The selector and baselines are not reoptimized inside resamples; the removed weighted mass can differ from the nominal row fraction. Neither population-wide significance nor independent dataset replication follows.\n\nSeparately, the stored aggregate seed-resampling interval for high-loss-relative gain is `[0.002381968563475412, 0.002381968563475412]`. Its equal endpoints reflect a deterministic contrast across recorded seeds, not certainty about data variation. The random-relative seed interval is `[0.007377907506332164, 0.008168732424939618]`. Ground those endpoints in the corresponding `final.metric.paired_gain_*_ci_*` IDs and explain their sampling unit in the main table caption.\n\nA short descriptive search table may list clean-Hessian-only selection `0.013240276724865765` and the two exact-refit configurations `0.01595532053579999` each, grounded respectively in `result.top_program_03`, `result.top_program_01`, and `result.top_program_02`. Show raw values only; no search trajectory, campaign delta, winner claim, final-ablation inference, or identical-behavior claim. Full configuration details belong after references.\n\n## Figure specifications and final-size geometry\nInspected files: `paper.sty`, the skill'\"'s \"'`templates/iclr/paper.tex`, and `VENUE.md`. The style sets **one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height**. Column width equals full text width; there is no two-column span to exploit. Use ordinary figures, not a two-column `figure*` assumption.\n\n### Figure 1 — `audit_overview` (required)\n- Purpose: early glance value for the shared object, frozen selection mechanism, and uncertainty boundary.\n- Generator: `generate-concept-figure`; one diagram panel, at most six main blocks and three semantic streams, a two-row/three-block arrangement within that panel.\n- Content: observed comparisons with Arena/NBA badges → regularized Bradley–Terry fit → frozen influence prefixes → bounded exact-refit selection → deletion and independent refits → dataset/budget contrasts. Attach a short note stating fixed-mask resampling without reselection. No grouped-deletion or adaptive attack imagery.\n- Placement: page 1 or 2; `0.98'\"\\\\textwidth\"'`, about 5.39 inches. Artwork about 2.6 inches tall; about 0.65 page including caption.\n- Readability: rendered labels at least 9 pt. Half-width placement would obscure the key mechanism distinctions.\n- Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes`; inspected studied implementation and `task/evaluate_final.py`. This is a conceptual diagram, not illustrative numerical evidence.\n\n### Figure 2 — `dataset_budget_comparison` (required)\n- Purpose: direct final comparisons exposing condition-dependent disruption.\n- Generator/type: matplotlib grouped-dot plot; two panels side by side, Arena and NBA; three budget categories; three method series.\n- Content: nominal deletion budgets 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%; studied selector, high-loss deletion, random deletion. Deterministic method markers have no fabricated error bars; show five random seed points and their mean in the same method color. Use a shared y scale, shared legend, and floor-budget explanation.\n- Placement: main results around page 5–6; `1.0'\"\\\\textwidth\"'` (5.5 inches). Artwork about 3.0 inches tall; about 0.70 page with caption; labels at least 9 pt.\n- Budget is a cross-sectional experimental condition, not temporal progression. Do not connect results into a search-history narrative or count controls as replicates.\n- Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes`; protocol definitions in `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n\n### Figure 3 — `conditional_resampling_contrasts` (required)\n- Purpose: expose conditional uncertainty and the dataset-specific baseline ordering in the main text.\n- Generator/type: matplotlib strip plot; two panels, one per comparator; two dataset categories per panel; one record series plus zero reference.\n- Content: the unique 32 stored paired gains per dataset for high-loss and random comparators. Use reproducible display jitter only; label the quantity “fixed-mask group-resampling gain.” No inferred full-procedure intervals or duplicated records.\n- Placement: immediately after Figure 2, around page 6–7; `1.0'\"\\\\textwidth\"'` (5.5 inches). Artwork about 2.7 inches tall; about 0.65 page with caption; labels at least 9 pt.\n- Density justifies full text width. Opposite comparator-relative signs and all conditioning limitations remain in the main body.\n- Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes`; `task/evaluate_final.py:final_uncertainty`.\n\n**Output contract for the later writer:** quantitative plots must have reproducible source, vector PDF and 300-DPI PNG; reference PDF from LaTeX whenever it exists. Prefer editable draw.io plus PDF/PNG for the concept figure when available. Raster-only full-width figures require at least 2400 px width; any later justified column-scale raster requires at least 1200 px. Never upscale: regenerate. No figures are rendered at this strategy stage. Use the concept-figure orchestration later rather than silently substituting a handmade overview.\n\nUse stable method colors in Figure 2 and clearly labeled dataset colors in Figure 3; the semantic roles differ and must not be conflated. No thresholded success-rate chart is needed.\n\n## Body budget and section order\nTarget approximately **8.5 body pages**, excluding references. Allocations include embedded figures/tables, are not measured pagination, and must be checked only at the later manuscript stage:\n\n| Component | Page-equivalent allocation |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Title and abstract | 0.5 |\n| Introduction, including early overview | 1.0 |\n| Related work | 0.6 |\n| Frozen-Prefix Ranking Audits | 1.6 |\n| Data, Metrics, and Uncertainty | 1.1 |\n| Dataset- and Budget-Dependent Disruption | 2.6 |\n| Discussion and Limitations | 0.8 |\n| Conclusion | 0.3 |\n\nUse three figures and two compact tables (aggregate final metrics; descriptive search configurations). The 8–9-page venue constraint is hard: an eight-page body must end at least 75% full. Do not alter margins, shrink labels, or pad. Spend space on the objective and mean-loss correction, precise baseline definitions, split/group limitations, direct budget contrasts, and the distinction between seed and group uncertainty. Do not turn page budget into permission for new claims or experiments.\n\nRequired reader-facing section order: Introduction; Related Work; Frozen-Prefix Ranking Audits; Data, Metrics, and Uncertainty; Dataset- and Budget-Dependent Disruption; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion; References; Supplementary Information.\n\n## Substantive supplementary plan\nPurpose: enable reproduction and audit of the narrow claim without hiding its decisive evidence after references. Use the same LaTeX source, references followed by a clear page, appendix mode, and a Supplementary Information heading. Incorporate separately supplied appendix-ranked posthoc analyses later; do not guess their rankings now.\n\n| Heading | Available grounded content | Source IDs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Retained-mean objective and frozen-prefix selection | Objective, correction, numerical fit details, six-contender rule, tie handling, compression/cache, warm starts, fallback, objective/metric mismatch. Implementations explain mechanisms, not unseen internal decisions. | `final.evidence.episodes` plus inspected candidate/evaluator code |\n| Data partitioning and reproducibility record | Pinned sources, content-group split, largest connected components, NBA copy filtering and historical-code limitations, hashes, seeds, CPU dependencies, staged-resource reproduction. Separate protocol metadata from measured findings. | `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status` plus staged manifests |\n| Numerical and row-order checks | Residual checks, no-deletion refit, constant-certificate rejection, reversed-row invariance, unverified candidate diagnostics, exact reported final outcome. | `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, `final.qualification.correct` |\n| Descriptive search configurations | Raw-score table and bounded expansion implementation; no final equivalence, runtime, independent ablation, or campaign-count claims. | `result.top_program_01`, `result.top_program_02`, `result.top_program_03` |\n| Conditional uncertainty protocol and stored records | Full unique group-weight records, fixed masks versus reselection, resampled mass, seed interval construction, limited resample count. Main text retains decisive signs. | `final.evidence.episodes`, the four `final.metric.paired_gain_*_ci_*` endpoint IDs |\n| LLM Usage | Supplied run logs show ideation, code generation, query planning, and manuscript assistance; disclose these roles without inventing model identities or human oversight. | Process provenance only; no numeric grounding ID claimed |\n\nThe normalized resources, evaluator, candidate implementation, requirements, and manifests are available. A complete raw-to-partition builder and all original raw archives are not staged. Do not promise a fully reconstructed raw-data pipeline, comparative runtime study, or new sensitivity analysis. Record actual available commands/dependencies at drafting time and distinguish rerunning staged resources from regeneration from originals. Follow the brief'\"'s raw-data handling and public-source restrictions.\n\n## Literature, anonymity, and claim discipline\nThe bibliography/provenance files were inspected. Retrieval includes irrelevant matches and does not resolve all exact planned identities for influence functions, finite-sample robustness, Arena, vote-rigging context, and NBA source documentation. Even the Bradley–Terry entries include duplicates. Do not cite unrelated search matches to fill a related-work section. Later, verify exact allowed bibliographic identities and actually consult their content before making literature claims; cite only synchronized BibTeX/provenance keys. No novelty priority claim is presently justified. No broad leaderboard searches or citation traversal; never try to identify the prohibited work. This strategy performs no network retrieval.\n\nPreserve anonymity: no author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying links, or internal filesystem URLs in the manuscript. Remove the template's final-copy and author scaffold during drafting, preserve the required watermark, and include a separate LLM Usage subsection. Do not invent a mandatory conference checklist that has not been supplied.\n\n## Headings and phrases to reject\nReject headings centered on **Search Progression, Campaign Phases, Milestones, First Valid Result, Timeline of Improvements, Experiment A, Experiment B, From Failure to Success, Winning Configuration**.\n\nReject **we then tried, first valid result, subsequent improvements, next-best, later variants, search winner** and unsupported **universally more robust, universally superior, state of the art, globally minimal deletion budget, causal effect of evaluation design, full-procedure bootstrap, five independent datasets, 160 independent bootstrap replicates**.\n\nKeep pipeline-native labels out of visible prose: **correctness rate, correct and eligible, eligible, combined_score, rubric, worker_01, worker_02**. Identifiers in this memo are private-only source maps, not exceptions allowing those labels in the paper.\n\nNo \"'`paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, final-paper validation, new experiment, or final-paper drafting belongs to this stage.\n'\"'''\noutputs={'paper_strategy.json':json.dumps(strategy,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\\\\n','paper_strategy.md':md}\npatch_text=''.join(''.join(difflib.unified_diff([],content.splitlines(keepends=True),fromfile='/dev/null',tofile=path)) for path,content in outputs.items())\nsubprocess.run(['sh','-c','apply_patch() { patch -p0; }; apply_patch'],input=patch_text,text=True,check=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport difflib,json,subprocess\nstrategy = {\n 'status':'ready',\n 'selected_prompt_family':'paper_standalone',\n 'recommended_title':'Exact-Refit Audits of Pairwise Ranking Sensitivity',\n 'central_question':'How do small, influence-ordered deletions affect regularized Bradley–Terry rankings, and how does their disruption compare with random and high-loss deletion across crowd preferences and historical sports outcomes?',\n 'central_thesis':'A frozen-prefix audit with bounded exact-refit contender selection yields greater aggregate rank disruption than the measured deletion baselines on the final partitions, but its advantage depends on dataset and deletion budget, with fixed-mask group resampling favoring high-loss deletion in Arena and the studied selector in NBA.',\n 'narrative_rationale':'The strongest evidence is a direct final comparison on two real-data settings plus a clearly delimited conditional uncertainty analysis, not an optimization success story. The shared object is sensitivity of the fitted ranking under row removal; Arena and NBA are condition-specific evidence blocks under that common question, not matched causal tests of evaluation design. Mechanism comes from inspected implementations; empirical effectiveness comes from independent evaluator refits. Search configurations provide descriptive supporting context only. Neither broad leaderboard fragility nor universal method superiority follows.',\n 'prior_paper_story_status':'recentered',\n 'edit_framing_decision':'Fresh framing, not an edit pass: no previous_paper directory, resolved edit-context files, aggregated directory, extra_tasks directory, or prior manuscript was staged. Recenter the broad source-task motivation and unexecuted grouped-deletion proposal on the measured anonymous-row, frozen-prefix audit. Compare three candidate framings: preserving a dependence-aware, multi-